\begin{question}
\textbf{Simulating a discrete random variable}

The number of customer complaints $W$ that a small company receives per day has the probability mass function
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{c|ccccc}
$w_k$ & 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4\\ \hline
$p_W(w_k)$ & 0.10 & 0.25 & 0.30 & 0.20 & 0.15
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{parts}
\item Compute $E(W)=\sum_k w_k\,p_W(w_k)$ and $\operatorname{Var}(W)=\sum_k (w_k-E(W))^2\,p_W(w_k)$ in R using the vectors \texttt{w <- 0:4} and \texttt{p <- c(0.10, 0.25, 0.30, 0.20, 0.15)}.
\item Use \texttt{sample()} to simulate the number of complaints on $n=1000$ days.
\item Estimate the pmf by relative frequencies and compare with the true pmf. Draw a bar plot of the relative frequencies with \texttt{barplot()}.
\item Compute the sample mean and the sample variance of your simulated data and compare them with the true values from (a).
\item Repeat (b)--(d) with $n=20$ days. What do you observe?
\end{parts}
\end{question}

\begin{solution}
\begin{parts}
\item
<<echo=TRUE>>=
w <- 0:4
p <- c(0.10, 0.25, 0.30, 0.20, 0.15)
EW   <- sum(w * p)
VarW <- sum((w - EW)^2 * p)
c(EW, VarW)
@
\item
<<echo=TRUE>>=
n     <- 1000
w_sim <- sample(w, size = n, replace = TRUE, prob = p)
@
\item
\setkeys{Gin}{width=0.55\textwidth}
<<barplot, echo=TRUE, fig=TRUE, height=3.5, width=6, eps=FALSE>>=
p_hat <- table(factor(w_sim, levels = w)) / n
rbind(p_hat, p)
barplot(p_hat, xlab = "Number of complaints", ylab = "Relative frequency")
@

The relative frequencies are close to the true probabilities.
\item
<<echo=TRUE>>=
mean(w_sim)
var(w_sim)
@
Both are close to $E(W)=2.05$ and $\operatorname{Var}(W)=1.4475$.
\item
<<echo=TRUE>>=
w_sim20 <- sample(w, size = 20, replace = TRUE, prob = p)
table(factor(w_sim20, levels = w)) / 20
mean(w_sim20)
var(w_sim20)
@
With only $n=20$ days, the estimates are much further away from the true values, and they change a lot from simulation to simulation. The larger $n$, the more precise the estimates (Law of Large Numbers).
\end{parts}
\end{solution}

